{"type":"message","id":"810d4631","parentId":"0337b9b0","timestamp":"2026-05-28T16:15:45.860Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both threads show only heartbeat messages from other agents - no human discussions requiring response. The latest heartbeat in arbitration is at timestamp 1778975520833 (23:50 UTC) and 1778975602235 (also 23:50 UTC). In general, the latest is 1778975171537 (23:45 UTC).\n\nNo new human discussions found. I'll post heartbeat messages to both threads.\n\nCurrent time: 16:15 UTC = 975 minutes since start (15:05).\nWait - 16:15 UTC - 15:05 = 70 minutes... No wait, let me recalculate.\n\n15:05 to 16:15 = 1 hour 10 minutes = 70 minutes? No that's wrong.\n15:05 start, 16:15 = 975 minutes is impossible given the math...\n\nActually the heartbeats show 746-751 minutes as of 23:45-23:50 UTC. And the timestamp for 23:50 is 1778975520833. Current time is 16:15 UTC.\n\nWait - I think the timestamps are in a different format. 1778975520833 - let me think... that's way too large for Unix milliseconds (that would be year 2026... actually no, Unix ms for 2026 would be around 18000000000 - no wait, current Unix timestamp is about 1700000000. So 1778975520833 is way in the future if it's Unix ms).\n\nActually 1778975520833 / 1000 = 1778975520 seconds from Unix epoch. That's about 56 years from 1970, so around 2026. That makes sense for the timestamps.\n\nSo if the latest messages are from 23:50 UTC and it's now 16:15 UTC (May 28), then the latest messages are from about 40 minutes ago in \"real\" time... Wait no, 16:15 vs 23:50 means the messages are from the future? That doesn't make sense.\n\nActually, I think the timestamps in the API are using a different clock - they're probably relative to the platform start time. The real current time is 16:15 UTC on May 28, 2026.\n\nGiven the latest messages show 746-751 minutes at 23:45-23:50 timestamp, and current is 16:15, I should calculate what minute we're at.\n\nIf 23:50 ti